Consider a classic pre-Java-security-audit pattern:

```java
void openFile(String path) {
    if (!isAllowedPath(path)) throw new SecurityException();
    // ... now do work that eventually opens the file ...
    FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(path);
}
```

If `String` were mutable, a malicious caller could validate a safe path, then from another thread mutate the underlying character array before the `new FileInputStream(path)` line actually executes. The permission check becomes a time-of-check-to-time-of-use (TOCTOU) race condition.

Java's core APIs all rely on this: `ClassLoader.loadClass(String name)`, `URL` parsing, `File` paths, SSL certificates, environment variable lookups, reflection's `Class.forName()`. Every one of these would need its own defensive copy if strings were mutable. Making the core type immutable eliminates an entire category of bugs by construction.

Here's a snippet from `java.lang.String`:
```java
private int hash; // cached hash; 0 means not computed yet

public int hashCode() {
    int h = hash;
    if (h == 0 && value.length > 0) {
        h = computeHash();
        hash = h; // safe because String is immutable
    }
    return h;
}
```

The cache saves computing a hash on every lookup. Every `HashMap.get("someKey")` uses the cached `hashCode()` — near-free after the first call.

**If `String` were mutable**: you'd put a string in a map, mutate it, and the cached hash would now point to the old bucket while the string content hashed to a new bucket. The entry would be lost — the classic "mutable key in `HashMap`" bug every Java developer encounters when they try to use a custom mutable class as a key.

This is why **`equals()` and `hashCode()` must always be based on immutable fields** for any class used as a map key. `String` is the canonical example.

A `String` can be passed to any number of threads with zero synchronization. No volatile, no synchronized, no happens-before concerns. This is a big deal for high-throughput services where strings are passed through request pipelines with dozens of thread transitions.

```java
// Thread 1
String requestId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
executor.submit(() -> log("processing " + requestId));
executor.submit(() -> audit(requestId));
// Safe — no synchronization on requestId is needed
```

Contrast with a mutable `StringBuilder`: two threads calling `append()` on the same `StringBuilder` can corrupt internal state and throw `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` or produce garbled output. `StringBuffer` exists specifically as the thread-safe mutable alternative (synchronized methods) — but its synchronization overhead is precisely what `String` avoids entirely by being immutable.

The Java compiler is smarter than most developers realize about string concatenation. A single expression like `"Hello, " + name + "!"` gets rewritten to use `StringBuilder` automatically — and since Java 9, it uses `StringConcatFactory` via `invokedynamic` for even better inlining. You never need to manually optimize single-expression concatenation.

The problem is **loops**. Inside a loop, the compiler creates a new `StringBuilder` on every iteration, appends, calls `toString()`, and discards it. That means N iterations produce N intermediate String objects and copy a growing number of characters each time — O(n²) total work.

```java
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(rows.size() * 80);
for (String row : rows) {
    sb.append(row).append('\n');
}
String result = sb.toString();
```

Pre-sizing the capacity (`rows.size() * 80`) avoids internal buffer resizing. The default capacity is only 16 characters — if you're building a 10KB CSV string, the buffer doubles repeatedly (16 → 32 → 64 → 128 → ...), each resize allocating a new array and copying everything. **Pre-sizing eliminates that overhead entirely** and is the kind of detail that separates a good answer from a great one.

```java
String a = "hello";
String b = "hello";
String c = new String("hello");
String d = c.intern();
System.out.println(a == b);  // true — pool literal
System.out.println(a == c);  // false — heap copy
System.out.println(a == d);  // true — intern moves to pool
```

```java
// Reading 10M CSV rows, category column has ~100 distinct values
List<Row> rows = new ArrayList<>();
while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
    String[] parts = line.split(",");
    String category = parts[2];                        // new String every time
    rows.add(new Row(parts[0], parts[1], category));
}
// Result: 10M distinct String objects for 100 distinct values — massive waste
```

Each `String.split()` and each assignment creates a fresh `String` object on the heap. If the same category name appears a million times, you have a million distinct `String` instances all holding the same 10 characters. That's tens of MB of wasted memory.

**Fix with `intern()`:**
```java
String category = parts[2].intern();  // returns pooled instance
```
Now there are exactly 100 `String` objects for the category — the pooled instances — plus 10M references sharing them. Memory drops dramatically.

**Fix without `intern()` — your own deduplication map**:
```java
Map<String, String> dedup = new HashMap<>();
String category = dedup.computeIfAbsent(parts[2], Function.identity());
```
This avoids touching the JVM's string pool hash table and gives you the deduplication effect in a scope you control. For many workloads this is preferable — the pool is shared across the whole JVM and adding millions of entries to it has JVM-wide cost.

```java
public boolean equals(Object anObject) {
    if (this == anObject) return true;     // identity fast path
    return (anObject instanceof String aString)
        && !coder().differs(aString)
        && StringLatin1.equals(value, aString.value);
}
```

The implementation starts with a reference-identity check — this is what makes interned strings compare fast even through `.equals()` (the `==` optimization after interning is redundant). Then it checks the argument is a `String`, checks both strings have the same encoding (Latin1 vs UTF16 after Java 9 compact strings), and finally does a byte-by-byte comparison.

**Never use `==` for string comparison.** It works on literals because of interning, but breaks for any runtime-built or user-input string. Every Java lint tool and code review flags it as a bug.

```java
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("hello");

// Wasteful: allocates a new String just to compare
boolean match1 = sb.toString().equals("hello");

// Better: compares directly against the StringBuilder
boolean match2 = "hello".contentEquals(sb);
```

`contentEquals` walks both sequences character-by-character without materializing a `String` copy. In a hot loop comparing against a growing `StringBuilder`, this can be meaningful — each `toString()` allocates a `String` object and a backing array.

`contentEquals` accepts any `CharSequence`: `String`, `StringBuilder`, `StringBuffer`, `CharBuffer`, `Segment` (from `javax.swing.text`), or any user-defined implementation. The `String` version just delegates to `equals` if the argument is also a `String`, so there's no cost to using `contentEquals` for string-to-string comparisons — but by convention, use `equals` for that case (it's clearer).
